WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.082 --> 00:00:01.355
- [Edna] Tamales mean warmth.

00:00:01.355 --> 00:00:03.342
- [Erwin] We were together as a family.

00:00:03.342 --> 00:00:06.276
- [Iván] This delicious treat,
that we all made it together.

00:00:06.276 --> 00:00:07.448
- [Julissa] I think of family.

00:00:07.448 --> 00:00:10.437
- Hi, I'm Edna and today I'm
making tamales from Mexico.

00:00:10.437 --> 00:00:13.570
When you are making tamales, you want to

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:16.360
soak the corn husks until they're pliable.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:19.719
My mom just fills up the sink
and throws them in overnight

00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:22.432
or throws them in in
the morning before work.

00:00:22.432 --> 00:00:23.738
Comes home, they're ready to go.

00:00:23.738 --> 00:00:25.404
Before I went to culinary
school, I was like,

00:00:25.404 --> 00:00:28.246
"Oh I know Mexican food.
Mexican food is this,

00:00:28.246 --> 00:00:31.079
and this type of ceviche
and these type of tamales."

00:00:31.079 --> 00:00:34.596
But then, I saw the variety,
like so many other families

00:00:34.596 --> 00:00:38.651
make tamales so many different
ways, and just Mexico alone,

00:00:38.651 --> 00:00:41.979
I couldn't imagine learning
every single different culture

00:00:41.979 --> 00:00:43.850
and the type of tamales they have.

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:46.609
Tamales mean warmth, I
mean we use them for every

00:00:46.609 --> 00:00:49.442
big occasion, holidays, birthdays.

00:00:49.532 --> 00:00:52.727
Every once in awhile, my
mom just randomly feels like

00:00:52.727 --> 00:00:55.411
making tamales and
those are the best days.

00:00:55.411 --> 00:00:59.472
So the meat, you want to
cook until it's pretty brown

00:00:59.472 --> 00:01:02.502
and the fat is all dried
up, for the most part.

00:01:02.502 --> 00:01:06.064
You want the sauce to do the
re-hydrating and softening.

00:01:06.064 --> 00:01:07.961
You want it to be fork-tender.

00:01:07.961 --> 00:01:11.344
Growing up, my mom used to save
me a couple of the dry bits

00:01:11.344 --> 00:01:14.072
of pork before adding the
sauce, but this was obviously

00:01:14.072 --> 00:01:17.246
after I had snuck a couple of them myself.

00:01:17.246 --> 00:01:20.542
I think what makes Mexico
really, really beautiful is that

00:01:20.542 --> 00:01:23.042
each state has such a variety.

00:01:23.491 --> 00:01:25.611
Honestly, the fillings are endless.

00:01:25.611 --> 00:01:28.032
You can go from savory to sweet.

00:01:28.032 --> 00:01:31.712
It's really up to you and
the family or country.

00:01:31.712 --> 00:01:35.728
You want to prepare the masa
itself by adding the lard,

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:38.794
baking soda, baking powder,
and a little bit of water.

00:01:38.794 --> 00:01:42.375
Mix it all by hand, because
that's the best way to do it.

00:01:42.375 --> 00:01:44.918
It's all about the feel with the masa.

00:01:44.918 --> 00:01:47.751
Masa is smooth, but firm and soft.

00:01:48.339 --> 00:01:50.786
As a chef, I think it's very
important to keep traditions

00:01:50.786 --> 00:01:53.730
alive and I love sharing
my family's recipes

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.957
because it keeps them going on forever.

00:01:55.957 --> 00:02:00.624
I think what makes my family's
tamales is really my mom.

00:02:00.687 --> 00:02:02.987
I feel like making tamales makes me just

00:02:02.987 --> 00:02:04.163
that much closer her.

00:02:04.163 --> 00:02:06.634
She is such a strong figure in my family.

00:02:06.634 --> 00:02:10.254
A strong and powerful, and the
cook, the everything, really.

00:02:10.254 --> 00:02:12.242
I hope to be like my mom one day.

00:02:12.242 --> 00:02:15.281
I had her send me recipes and
she made little clips of her

00:02:15.281 --> 00:02:18.647
making it, little
tutorials for me from far.

00:02:18.647 --> 00:02:21.330
So, I hope to make her proud of these.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:23.821
(lively music)

00:02:23.821 --> 00:02:25.797
- Hey there. I'm Iván,
and today I'm making the

00:02:25.797 --> 00:02:27.450
Puerto Rican pastel de yuca.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:30.267
Pasteles de yuca are little
pockets of yuca dough

00:02:30.267 --> 00:02:33.220
filled with pork,
wrapped in banana leaves.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:36.573
So what makes Puerto Rican
pasteles de yuca so special

00:02:36.573 --> 00:02:40.025
is that we cook the pork with
so much flavor and seasoning.

00:02:40.025 --> 00:02:43.321
And also the way we use yuca
as our dough, just flavor

00:02:43.321 --> 00:02:45.464
and seasoning, that's kind of like the key

00:02:45.464 --> 00:02:47.692
of a delicious Puerto Rican meal.

00:02:47.692 --> 00:02:50.201
The very first time I had
a pastel de yuca was when

00:02:50.201 --> 00:02:53.738
my grandma made it for the
family at a Christmas Eve dinner.

00:02:53.738 --> 00:02:56.407
It was the most delicious
thing I've ever had.

00:02:56.407 --> 00:03:00.004
But definitely this recipe
is made by my mom, but I feel

00:03:00.004 --> 00:03:02.587
like my dad usually
always tries to take over

00:03:02.587 --> 00:03:05.417
and then he's the one that
says "Oh, look what I made."

00:03:05.417 --> 00:03:07.758
But yeah, I feel like it's a
combination of both working

00:03:07.758 --> 00:03:10.493
together in the kitchen and
I feel like that's so lovely,

00:03:10.493 --> 00:03:12.152
because it makes it even more special.

00:03:12.152 --> 00:03:14.889
One of the best memories that
I have making pasteles de yuca

00:03:14.889 --> 00:03:17.676
with my parents are getting
messy when I was a child.

00:03:17.676 --> 00:03:20.592
I really didn't know how to
cook, so I would try to help,

00:03:20.592 --> 00:03:23.410
but instead I would just
start playing with the dough,

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:25.973
and that was pretty much my help.

00:03:25.973 --> 00:03:28.435
So now it's finally time
to assemble our delicious

00:03:28.435 --> 00:03:30.727
pasteles de yuca, but
first we have to heat up

00:03:30.727 --> 00:03:32.748
the banana leaves so that they're pliable.

00:03:32.748 --> 00:03:35.383
So some families add
raisins and capors to their

00:03:35.383 --> 00:03:38.593
pasteles de yuca and some
even add garbanzo beans,

00:03:38.593 --> 00:03:41.303
which I've never had a
pastel with garbanzo beans,

00:03:41.303 --> 00:03:42.574
but I guess that's a thing.

00:03:42.574 --> 00:03:44.937
My family never adds any of those things.

00:03:44.937 --> 00:03:47.793
We like to just let the
meat speak for itself.

00:03:47.793 --> 00:03:50.477
So whenever I bite into a
pastel de yuca, the flavor

00:03:50.477 --> 00:03:54.529
immediately hits and it's
just like a tangy, salty,

00:03:54.529 --> 00:03:57.249
but also a little bit
sweet from the yuca flavor,

00:03:57.249 --> 00:03:58.591
and it's just so delicious.

00:03:58.591 --> 00:04:01.726
And then you bite even more
into the meat and then it's like

00:04:01.726 --> 00:04:05.529
a burst of flavor with the
seasoning and all the spices

00:04:05.529 --> 00:04:08.632
and it's just -- so I would
add ketchup to my pasteles

00:04:08.632 --> 00:04:11.192
and my dad, especially,
would get so mad because he

00:04:11.192 --> 00:04:14.502
would say, "I spend so much
time giving this pastel flavor

00:04:14.502 --> 00:04:16.568
and then your gonna spread
ketchup all over it.

00:04:16.568 --> 00:04:18.911
That's so disrespectful,"
and I would just laugh,

00:04:18.911 --> 00:04:20.700
because I was like, "Well
I like it with ketchup,

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:22.491
so I'm gonna eat it with ketchup.

00:04:22.491 --> 00:04:25.613
I love pasteles de yuca so
much I feel like it's that

00:04:25.613 --> 00:04:28.665
tiny piece of home that I
get to keep, even if I live

00:04:28.665 --> 00:04:31.412
miles and miles away from
home, so it definitely brings

00:04:31.412 --> 00:04:34.411
all my family together and
I feel like that's something

00:04:34.411 --> 00:04:38.161
very important for Puerto
Ricans, in general.

00:04:39.038 --> 00:04:41.236
Oh my God. My mom will be so proud.

00:04:41.236 --> 00:04:44.475
- Hey. I'm Erwin, and today
I'm gonna show you how to make

00:04:44.475 --> 00:04:45.755
Guatemalan paches.

00:04:45.755 --> 00:04:49.330
Paches are Guatemalan tamales,
these are made with tomatoes.

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:51.196
Wait, not tomatoes. Potatoes.

00:04:51.196 --> 00:04:52.431
(laughs)

00:04:52.431 --> 00:04:55.880
The paches are the best
tamales in Latin America.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:59.903
What I remember, I was around
six years old the first time

00:04:59.903 --> 00:05:03.271
when I try paches and
they were really good.

00:05:03.271 --> 00:05:06.538
You never can forget this taste,
because when you're eating

00:05:06.538 --> 00:05:09.799
a pache, the taste is like
the most beautiful thing

00:05:09.799 --> 00:05:12.265
in the world and it's so delicious.

00:05:12.265 --> 00:05:14.799
So the paches are really important for me

00:05:14.799 --> 00:05:17.668
because remember me when I was little.

00:05:17.668 --> 00:05:21.047
All the family was together
and we always ate paches

00:05:21.047 --> 00:05:23.891
on Saturdays, which is something like masa

00:05:23.891 --> 00:05:25.931
with family doughs in Gatamala.

00:05:25.931 --> 00:05:27.208
It's really special.

00:05:27.208 --> 00:05:29.711
It's something cultural that we always do.

00:05:29.711 --> 00:05:31.269
So I live in Los Angeles.

00:05:31.269 --> 00:05:34.249
My wife and my kids live
so far away from here,

00:05:34.249 --> 00:05:35.454
they live in Guatemala.

00:05:35.454 --> 00:05:38.058
And I'm real excited because
I'm gonna see then soon.

00:05:38.058 --> 00:05:39.733
And I know the are excited, as well.

00:05:39.733 --> 00:05:41.492
We make a lot of tamales.

00:05:41.492 --> 00:05:43.875
We make like more than 100 tamales.

00:05:43.875 --> 00:05:47.060
We are giving to the neighbors,
so it's really special,

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:49.593
because when you're taking
tamales to your neighbors

00:05:49.593 --> 00:05:51.718
they now so much spirit into your family.

00:05:51.718 --> 00:05:55.045
One thing I love about my
mom is when she's cooking

00:05:55.045 --> 00:05:57.860
you know it's always
delicious, and she doesn't take

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:00.843
the compliment when you say,
"Oh it's really delicious."

00:06:00.843 --> 00:06:03.650
And she's always, "Oh, I
forgot to put something on it."

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:06.477
She never takes the compliment
of the delicious food

00:06:06.477 --> 00:06:07.310
that she makes.

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:10.562
All the time I'm always asking
my mom to make me some paches

00:06:10.562 --> 00:06:14.495
and she's always like, "Oh
yeah, I can do it for you,

00:06:14.495 --> 00:06:17.273
but you guys need to help me
because it's a lot to do."

00:06:17.273 --> 00:06:19.704
And I'm always like, "Of
course, Mom. I'll help you."

00:06:19.704 --> 00:06:22.872
And it's really funny when
we're really packing all

00:06:22.872 --> 00:06:25.144
the paches, because I don't have practice

00:06:25.144 --> 00:06:26.643
when I'm making the paches.

00:06:26.643 --> 00:06:29.230
One, it's too big and the
other one is too small,

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:31.976
and she's always like, "Do
the right size, do the right

00:06:31.976 --> 00:06:35.005
size," and she's always
made at me because of that.

00:06:35.005 --> 00:06:37.905
When she's packing them she's
always like, "Oh, I forgot

00:06:37.905 --> 00:06:40.724
if I put meat in that
one. I don't remember."

00:06:40.724 --> 00:06:43.083
So she'll start all over
again and it's funny

00:06:43.083 --> 00:06:45.433
because it happens, when you're
doing paches you're like,

00:06:45.433 --> 00:06:47.369
"Oh I don't know if I forgot the meat,"

00:06:47.369 --> 00:06:49.347
which is really important in the paches.

00:06:49.347 --> 00:06:51.735
It was really fun making
all the tamales together

00:06:51.735 --> 00:06:55.686
because it was always
something for somebody to do.

00:06:55.686 --> 00:06:59.010
That way you can make the
tamales faster, that way you can

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:01.480
not get too tired doing it by yourself.

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:04.779
Now because we were working
all together making the tamales

00:07:04.779 --> 00:07:09.696
that's why it's a really special
moment for me to remember.

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:11.843
Mmmm.

00:07:12.333 --> 00:07:13.166
Mm-hmm.

00:07:14.177 --> 00:07:16.133
- Hi. I'm Julissa, and today I'm making

00:07:16.133 --> 00:07:18.300
Dominican pastel en hojas.

00:07:19.002 --> 00:07:22.871
I feel like when I bite
into a pastel and I get that

00:07:22.871 --> 00:07:26.913
first bite, it's like a hint
of savory, but then you get

00:07:26.913 --> 00:07:31.830
that little, little sweetness
in the back of your pallette.

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:34.993
Dominican food is so flavorful.

00:07:35.224 --> 00:07:37.693
We don't like spicy, contrary
to the belief of everyone

00:07:37.693 --> 00:07:40.001
thinking, "Latinos enjoy spicy food."

00:07:40.001 --> 00:07:43.584
We don't. Our spices
come from just flavor.

00:07:43.949 --> 00:07:46.734
You know, all around Latin
America everybody has a lot of

00:07:46.734 --> 00:07:50.243
the same dishes, but everyone
also puts their own twist

00:07:50.243 --> 00:07:54.243
on it, and for us we use
platanos, which is like

00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:57.350
life in Dominican Republic,
that's all we eat.

00:07:57.350 --> 00:08:01.100
So we just wrap this
plantain up in some delicious

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:03.267
ground beef and that's it.

00:08:03.801 --> 00:08:06.303
It's your snack, it's your
dinner, it's your side.

00:08:06.303 --> 00:08:08.594
It's whatever you want to have.

00:08:08.594 --> 00:08:11.258
Whenever it's made, you know
it's because of the holidays

00:08:11.258 --> 00:08:15.526
and you know that it just
brings warmth and happiness to

00:08:15.526 --> 00:08:17.153
the family and to me.

00:08:17.153 --> 00:08:19.051
That's usually what I think of.

00:08:19.051 --> 00:08:21.410
You know, I never get tired
of saying why I'm proud

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:22.583
of being Domican.

00:08:22.583 --> 00:08:25.779
As many times as people ask
me, I love talking about it.

00:08:25.779 --> 00:08:30.279
Just our culture of being
such happy and proud people,

00:08:30.883 --> 00:08:34.092
and being proud Latinos
and embracing every part

00:08:34.092 --> 00:08:35.295
of that journey.

00:08:35.295 --> 00:08:38.754
The laughter that we
always bring, the humor.

00:08:38.754 --> 00:08:40.796
What's really interesting is I didn't like

00:08:40.796 --> 00:08:42.542
pastel en hojas when I was younger.

00:08:42.542 --> 00:08:43.393
(laughs)

00:08:43.393 --> 00:08:46.084
I was like, "Ew, that's
gross," without trying it.

00:08:46.084 --> 00:08:49.236
And as I got older, I
remember it was one Christmas

00:08:49.236 --> 00:08:52.569
at my Tia Petra's house, and I loved it.

00:08:52.705 --> 00:08:54.667
We put ketchup on it after.

00:08:54.667 --> 00:08:58.750
For me, ketchup on it was
like the cherry on top.

00:08:59.777 --> 00:09:03.077
Now I can't go a Christmas
without having it, which is

00:09:03.077 --> 00:09:06.244
so funny because I did a complete 360.

00:09:08.348 --> 00:09:09.181
Mmm.

00:09:09.281 --> 00:09:10.114
Mm-mm-mm!

00:09:10.612 --> 00:09:11.695
This is good.

00:09:12.979 --> 00:09:14.718
- [Edna] They just always
remind me of my mom.

00:09:14.718 --> 00:09:17.239
- [Erwin] They're delicious.
You really have to try them.

00:09:17.239 --> 00:09:19.749
- [Iván] It was the most
delicious thing I've ever had.

00:09:19.749 --> 00:09:21.899
- [Julissa] This is gonna
ake me extremely happy.

00:09:21.899 --> 00:09:22.982
[Man] Oh yes.

00:09:23.269 --> 00:09:25.852
(lively music)

